# PRIMEMET — environment variables
#
# Nothing here is required to run the site in development. A production build requires
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL (see below) and fails without it.
#
# What the running application actually reads today: NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL and MONGODB_URI.
# Everything else is listed so the deployment can be prepared, and is unread until the feature it
# belongs to exists. There is no authentication and no mail integration yet.
#
# Copy to .env.local for local work. .env* is git-ignored except this example file.
# Never commit real credentials — placeholders only, as below.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Active now (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute site origin used for canonical URLs, Open Graph, sitemap.xml and robots.txt.
# Development (`next dev`): optional; falls back to http://localhost:3000.
# Production (`next build` / `next start`): REQUIRED. Must be an https origin with no path
# and must not be localhost/127.0.0.1/::1 — the build fails otherwise (no fallback).
# Production value: https://primemet.in
# The value below is for local development only.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MongoDB Atlas — primary production database. Server-side ONLY.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by src/lib/db.ts, src/app/api/health/route.ts and scripts/db-init.mjs. There is no
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, so Next.js never inlines it into a browser bundle. Never commit the real value.
#
# Obtain: MongoDB Atlas → Database → Connect → Drivers. Use the SRV string and add
#   /primemet?retryWrites=true&w=majority&appName=primemet
#
# TWO identities exist, and they are not interchangeable:
#   primemet_app    — the runtime user. This is the value the application is configured with.
#   primemet_owner  — initialization only, for `npm run db:init`. Never set it in Vercel.
# Both roles are created by `npm run db:provision -- --apply` (db/provision-roles.mjs).
# Run `npm run db:check-role` with the application URI to prove the deployed identity is not the
# owner: it fails if the connected identity holds an owner role.
#
# The example below therefore shows the APPLICATION user, not the owner.
# MONGODB_URI=mongodb+srv://primemet_app:<password>@<cluster>.mongodb.net/primemet?retryWrites=true&w=majority

# Database name inside the cluster. Optional; defaults to "primemet".
# MONGODB_DB=primemet

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEPRECATED — PostgreSQL. No longer read by the application.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Prisma schema and the seven migrations are retained as historical reference
# (prisma/README.md). Prisma tooling reads prisma/.env, not this file. Setting these has no
# effect on the running application, which uses MONGODB_URI.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.[project-ref]:[password]@[region].pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres
# DIRECT_URL=postgresql://postgres.[project-ref]:[password]@[region].pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stage 2 — authentication session signing. Not read by any code yet.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# AUTH_SECRET=replace-with-a-random-32-byte-value

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stage 2 — outbound mail (Zoho Mail SMTP). Not read by any code yet.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ZOHO_SMTP_HOST=smtp.zoho.com
# ZOHO_SMTP_PORT=465
# ZOHO_SMTP_USER=enquiries@example.com
# ZOHO_SMTP_PASSWORD=app-specific-password-placeholder
# MAIL_FROM=PRIMEMET <enquiries@example.com>
